INT. CAPE HALLOW LIGHTHOUSE - SPIRAL STAIR - DAWN

MIRA VOSS, 71, spine straight as the rail, climbs with a rag and a tin of brass polish tucked under one arm. Gulls needle the wind below. The great lens throws pink dawn light across the curved walls in slow, deliberate sweeps.

MIRA
(to the lens, fondly)
Still slower than you used to be. Aren't we both.

She reaches the gallery, sets the tin down, begins working salt crust from the brass fittings.

EXT. LIGHTHOUSE DOCK - LATER

A small ferry idles at the pier. PEARL VOSS, 24, hair wind-whipped, hauls a duffel bag off the gangplank. Mira waits at the end of the dock, wiping polish from her hands with a rag.

PEARL
You said you'd stopped climbing the tower yourself.

MIRA
I said I'd stopped enjoying it. Different thing entirely.

Pearl laughs, drops the bag, hugs her grandmother hard enough to rock her back on her heels.

EXT. LIGHTHOUSE ACCESS ROAD - AFTERNOON

A grader truck and two SUVs idle at the gate. HOLLIS KADE, 40s, silver windbreaker, clipboard, surveys the lighthouse like a man pricing a used car. A SURVEYOR drives a stake into the grass twenty feet from the tower.

KADE
Ms. Voss. Coastal Ridge Development. I take it the county called ahead.

MIRA
The county called ahead about a resurfacing permit. Not this.

KADE
(handing over a folder)
Reclassification went through in March. Air rights, land use, all of it. You've got sixty days before the permit finalizes.

MIRA
Finalizes into what.

KADE
Eleven oceanfront villas. The tower comes down first — obstructs the view corridor.

INT. LIGHTHOUSE KITCHEN - NIGHT

Mira and Pearl spread Kade's folder across the table. Permits, survey stakes, a demolition timeline circled in red.

PEARL
Sixty days. That's — Grandma, that's nothing.

MIRA
It's enough to make noise. County council meets Thursday.

PEARL
And if noise isn't enough?

MIRA
(closing the folder)
Then we find out what is.

INT. TOWN HALL - EVENING

A packed room. Mira stands at a podium, voice steady over restless murmurs. Kade sits in the front row, unbothered, tapping a pen.

MIRA
That light has kept ships off Hallow Reef for eighty years. No villa view is worth what it's replacing.

KADE
(standing, easy smile)
With respect — the light's automated backup two towns over does the same job, cheaper, and nobody has to climb a hundred and twelve stairs to polish it.

Scattered laughter. Mira's jaw tightens. A COUNCIL MEMBER bangs a gavel, adjourns without a vote.

EXT. LIGHTHOUSE ACCESS ROAD - DAY

Surveyors hammer stakes closer to the foundation now. Kade watches from his SUV, window down.

KADE
You can keep showing up to meetings, Ms. Voss. Eminent domain doesn't take a vote.

MIRA
Neither does spite. You could've bought land anywhere on this coast.

KADE
I did. This parcel's just the one with the best light.

He rolls the window up.

INT. LIGHTHOUSE WORKSHOP - NIGHT

Pearl, surrounded by Tomas's old files, finds a half-finished historic-landmark application in his handwriting, dated years back, never submitted — missing one signature and a filing fee.

PEARL
He started this. He never finished it.

MIRA
Your grandfather started a lot of paperwork he hated.

PEARL
(already writing)
Then I'll finish the part he hated.

EXT. COUNTY CLERK'S OFFICE - DAY

Pearl, out of breath, slides the completed application across the counter. The CLERK checks a wall clock: 4:52.

CLERK
Filing window closes at five. Cutting it close.

Through the glass door, Kade's ASSISTANT enters behind her, folder in hand, waiting his turn at the same counter. Pearl watches him watch her.

EXT. LIGHTHOUSE ACCESS ROAD - DAWN

A bulldozer idles at the gate. Kade stands beside it, arms crossed. Mira plants herself on the bottom step of the tower, immovable.

KADE
Ms. Voss, step aside. This isn't a discussion anymore.

MIRA
It wasn't one when you started, either.

KADE
(to the operator)
Bring it forward.

The engine growls, treads grinding gravel toward the steps. Mira doesn't move.

EXT. LIGHTHOUSE ACCESS ROAD - CONTINUOUS

Pearl's truck skids up the road. She runs the last stretch waving a stamped county document over her head.

PEARL
Stop the dozer! Historic landmark status — filed yesterday, four-fifty-eight!

The operator kills the engine before Kade can answer. Kade snatches the page, reads it twice, jaw working.

KADE
(low)
You had two minutes.

MIRA
We had enough.

Kade turns, waves his crew off.

EXT. LIGHTHOUSE GALLERY - SUNSET

Mira and Pearl sit on the gallery floor, backs against the rail, wrung out. The bulldozer's gone from the road below.

PEARL
Four-fifty-eight.

MIRA
(eyes closed, almost laughing)
Don't ever tell me that again.

EXT. LIGHTHOUSE GALLERY - DUSK (WEEKS LATER)

A small bronze plaque now reads CAPE HALLOW LIGHT — HISTORIC LANDMARK, 2026. Mira watches the beam sweep the water. Pearl climbs up with two mugs of coffee, handing one over.

PEARL
County wants an assistant keeper of record. Paid position.

MIRA
(taking the mug)
Only if you can beat my sevens.

PEARL
I already do.

They watch the light turn together.
